{"records":[{"id":"8zg72dh86w","title":"Combinatorial gene essentiality and pharmacological profiling uncovers enhancer-mediated synthetic lethal interactions with Mediator Kinase","description":"This datasets contain all the original western blots published in \"Combinatorial gene essentiality and pharmacological profiling uncovers enhancer-mediated synthetic lethal interactions with Mediator Kinase\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-12-15T13:01:12.712Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8zg72dh86w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8zg72dh86w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Claire","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ron","last_name":"Firestein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dhanya","last_name":"Sooraj","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aleks","last_name":"Guanizo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chunhua","last_name":"Wan","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Marius","last_name":"Dannappel ","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mk7z7st438","title":"ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition. Rainey et al","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is required for activation of licensed origins of replication during the process of DNA duplication. CDC7 kinase inhibitors interfere with this process, limiting both DNA synthesis and cell proliferation.\n\nAim:\nIdentify genes responsible for limiting cell proliferation in response to inhibition of CDC7 using small molecule ATP-competitive inhibitors.\n\nStrategy:\nA CRISPR/Cas9 loss of function screen was performed to identify genes that upon loss allow bypass of a proliferation blockade imposed by the CDC7 inhibitor, XL413.\n\nData:\nRepresents source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the Manuscript 'ATR restrains DNA synthesis and mitotic catastrophe in response to CDC7 inhibition', Rainey et al.\n\nData collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-19T20:26:38.851Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mk7z7st438","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mk7z7st438"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Rainey","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Replication","Cell Signaling","Genetic Screening"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7rgn4p4cvp","title":"PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors","description":"Background:\nCDC7 kinase is crucial for the initiation of DNA replication and is important for replication stress responses. Several ATP-competitive CDC7 inhibitors (CDC7is) are being developed as possible cancer therapeutics. CDC7 inhibition causes a dose-dependent delay in S-phase progression and mild activation of the S-phase checkpoint pathway, which further limits origin firing through ATR activation.\n\nMethodology:\nTo identify genes that modulate the cellular responses to CDC7is, we established a CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide loss-of-function screen. The following data represent the closer investigation of one of these genes, PTBP1, using RNA sequencing, flow cytometry, immunofluorescence analysis and additional techniques as described in the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors'.\n\nData:\nData represent source data for main figures and supplemental items in support of the manuscript 'PTBP1 enforces ATR-CHK1 signalling determining the potency of CDC7 inhibitors', Göder et al. Data collection, interpretation and findings are addressed in the manuscript.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T16:30:16.52Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7rgn4p4cvp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7rgn4p4cvp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anja","last_name":"Göder","orcid":"0000-0001-9743-1656"},{"first_name":"Corrado","last_name":"Santocanale","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/501100001602","name":"Science Foundation Ireland","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","Cell Cycle","Cell Cycle Checkpoint","RNA Splicing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8sgbfwz2mh","title":"A latent U-STAT1 reservoir primes macrophages for second-hit immune amplification","description":"Severe inflammatory syndromes, including secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (sHLH), cancer immunotherapy-associated HLH-like hyperinflammation, acute exacerbation of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (AE-IPF), and systemic inflammatory multiorgan failure, can progress rapidly, yet how limited immune activation escalates into severe disease remains unclear. Across three two-hit models, we identified a conserved STAT1-high macrophage state. Temporally resolved poly(I:C)–LPS experiments showed that unphosphorylated STAT1 (U-STAT1) accumulated during clinically silent priming and became phosphorylated at Y701 after the second hit, coincident with lethal inflammation. Reservoir formation required interferon-γ signaling, NAD⁺ preservation, and PARP2-mediated PARylation of STAT1 at D593. In human IPF, alveolar macrophages displayed a STAT1-high, phosphorylation-low state. Both D593 mutation and pharmacological PARP inhibition attenuated lethal inflammation across models. These findings identify a D593-modified U-STAT1 reservoir that links prior immune priming to subsequent inflammatory amplification and defines a potentially targetable window of susceptibility, with implications for predicting and preventing secondary hyperinflammation, including that associated with cancer immunotherapy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-01T09:23:49.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8sgbfwz2mh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8sgbfwz2mh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chun-te","last_name":"Ho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx5jtyxtxr","title":"Increased lactate secretion by cancer cells sustains non-cell-autonomous adaptive resistance to MET and EGFR targeted therapies. Apicella et al.","description":"Microenvironment is known to influence cancer drug response and sustain resistance to therapies targeting receptor-tyrosine kinases. However if and how tumor microenvironment can be altered during treatment, contributing to resistance onset is not known. Here we show that, under prolonged treatment with tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs), EGFR- or MET-addicted cancer cells displayed a metabolic shift towards increased glycolysis and lactate production. We identified secreted lactate as the key molecule able to instruct Cancer Associated Fibroblasts (CAFs) to produce Hepatocyte Growth Factor (HGF) in a NF-KB dependent manner. Increased HGF, activating MET-dependent signaling in cancer cells, sustained resistance to TKIs. Functional or pharmacological targeting of molecules involved in the lactate axis, such as lactate dehydrogenase or the lactate transporters MCT4 and MCT1, abrogated in vivo resistance, demonstrating the crucial role of this metabolite in the adaptive process. \nThis non-cell-autonomous, adaptive resistance mechanism was observed in NSCLC patients progressed on EGFR TKIs, demonstrating the clinical relevance of our findings and opening novel scenarios in the challenge to drug resistance.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-20T07:35:09.484Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx5jtyxtxr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx5jtyxtxr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Apicella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elisa ","last_name":"Giannoni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephany","last_name":"Fiore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karin","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Fernández-Pérez ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claudio","last_name":"Isella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlotta","last_name":"Granchi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Filippo","last_name":"Minutolo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonino","last_name":"Sottile","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paolo","last_name":"Comoglio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Enzo","last_name":"Medico","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Filippo","last_name":"Pietrantonio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Volante","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diego","last_name":"Pasini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paola","last_name":"Chiarugi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silvia","last_name":"Giordano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simona ","last_name":"Corso","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mechanism of Resistance","Molecular Mechanism of Cancer","Translational Oncology"],"journal_issn":["15504131"]},{"id":"fpwtnn9yp8","title":"CancerSSDTs","description":"CancerSSDTs\nThis repository contains data, codes, and supplementary information used to identify strictly-selective drug targets for 13 cancers.\n\nRequirements\nThe following tools are needed:\n\nCOBRA Toolbox\nrFASTCORMICS\nCPLEX v12.9.0 or any CPLEX version compatible with MATLAB.\n\nTo Obtain Strictly Selective Lethal Sets, Follow the Steps Below:\nInitialize COBRA toolbox:\ninitCobraToolbox\nUse rFASTCORMICS to build normal and cancer context-specific models for each tissue. The parameters for this step are provided in BuildCSM.m. Provided models are available in Contex_Specific_Models:\nBuildCSM.m\nUse RapidSL to identify single and synthetic lethal drug targets for all cancers. A sample script is provided in RunRapidSL.m. All identified selective and non-selective solutions are available in All_Identified_Synthetic_Lethal_Non_Selective:\nRunRapidSL.m\nUse the FindSSDTs.m function to extract strictly-selective drug targets for each cancer. A sample use of this function is provided in IdentifySelectives.m:\nIdentifySelectives.m\nAll SSDTs identified in this study are available in the Supplementary_File_S1:\nThis repository represents the data related to the following submitted manuscript:\nManshadi et al.,\"Higher order synthetic lethals are keys to minimizing cancer treatment effects on normal cells\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-13T08:41:33.35Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fpwtnn9yp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fpwtnn9yp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"mehdi","last_name":"Dehghan Manshadi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Payam","last_name":"Setoodeh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amin Reza","last_name":"Rajabzadeh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amin","last_name":"Ramezani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Habil","last_name":"Zare","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Software"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ybmr387wyv","title":"Aberrant accumulation of Kras-dependent enhancer RNAs during tumor progression renders cancer cells susceptible to PAF1 depletion","description":"KRAS is the most commonly mutated oncogene in human cancer and mutant KRAS is responsible for over 90% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), the most lethal cancer. Here, we show that RNA polymerase II associated factor 1 complex (PAF1C) is specifically required for the survival of PDAC but not normal adult pancreatic cells. We show that PAF1C maintains cancer cell genomic stability by restraining the over accumulation of enhancer RNAs (eRNAs) and PROMPTs driven by mutant Kras. Loss of PAF1C leads to cancer-specific lengthening and accumulation of pervasive transcripts on chromatin, and concomitant aberrant R-loop formation and DNA damage, which in turn trigger cell death. We go on to demonstrate that the global transcriptional hyperactivation driven by Kras signaling during tumorigenesis underlies the specific demand for PAF1C by cancer cells. Our work provides insights in how enhancer transcription hyperactivation causes general transcription factor addiction during tumorigenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-09T11:01:29.655Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ybmr387wyv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ybmr387wyv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mo","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinhong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiangzheng","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Du","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Di","last_name":"Zou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xun","last_name":"Lan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"David","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qianwen","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2020YFA0509402"},{"id":null,"name":"Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"92068114"},{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2021YFA1300100"}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Tumor Biology","Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bk9m6kf8s5","title":"The Second Mitochondrial Activator of Caspases (SMAC) regulates growth, inflammation and mitochondrial integrity in cancer cells","description":"SMAC is a mitochondrial intermembrane space protein, which is released during apoptosis and whose known function is antagonism of inhibitor of apoptosis proteins in the cytosol, to facilitate caspase activation. Recent data suggest that SMAC can also be released by sub-lethal signals in the apoptosis pathway, in the absence of cell death. We here explored potential functions of SMAC in non-apoptotic cells. We found that a portion of SMAC is spontaneously released into the cytosol in the absence of apoptosis, regulated by the BCL-2-family proteins BAX and BAK and the fission GTPase DRP1. In cancer cell lines, SMAC was required for the activation of caspases in lethal and non-lethal conditions, while this contribution to caspase-activation was much smaller in non-malignant fibroblast lines. In cells with high levels of cytosolic SMAC, SMAC deficiency reduced in vitro migration, invasion and anchorage-independent growth as well as metastasis in a xenograft model in zebrafish. SMAC-deficient cells further showed a reduced activity in interferon signalling, associated with reduced cytosolic presence of mitochondrial DNA and activation of the stimulator of interferon genes (STING), and SMAC expression levels correlated with interferon-induced genes in cancer data sets. We further found that SMAC can regulate mitochondrial morphology and integrity. Finally, high gene-expression of SMAC was associated with poor prognosis in patients of several cancer types. These results identify SMAC as a regulator of inflammation and growth behaviour of cancer cells. They further report a mitochondrial function of SMAC and demonstrate a role of SMAC in human cancer biology across several cancer entities.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-11T14:57:32.002Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bk9m6kf8s5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bk9m6kf8s5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tarek","last_name":"Amer","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/02q83sc19","name":"Wilhelm Sander Stiftung","grant_id":"2019.019.1"}],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8wsxb5cdbn","title":"Data for: Is Intelligence Associated with Mortality from Lethal Force by Law Enforcement?","description":"This research data contains state estimates for mortality from lethal force, Intelligence, well-being, divorce, among many other variables. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-04T03:50:11.034Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8wsxb5cdbn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8wsxb5cdbn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Adam","last_name":"Alexander","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Ward","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weiyu","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Social Sciences","Cognitive Psychology","Criminology","Human Intelligence","Law Enforcement"],"journal_issn":["01602896"]}],"total_records":128734,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":144,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":128734,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":128233,"restricted":66,"embargoed":435},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2151},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1375},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":592},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":447},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":430},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":358},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":317},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":262}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":126119},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":63},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":892},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":822},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":409},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}